# std/INSTALL — Installing the Standard Library

File: std/docs/install.oot

This file describes how to install the openOODA standard library
via opm, and what version compatibility contract you must respect.

## 1. Install via opm

The standard library is a separate package. Install it with opm:

  opm install openOODA/std

This pulls the current v1.0.0 release into your local opm cache
and registers it in the lockfile. The compiler (oodac) resolves
`import "std/..."` paths against this cache. There is no
environment variable to set. There is no path to add. opm handles
the rest.

To pin a specific version:

  opm install openOODA/std@1.0.0

To upgrade within the same major:

  opm update openOODA/std

To verify the install:

  opm list | grep openOODA/std
  opm show openOODA/std

The `show` command prints the resolved version, the sha pin, and
the cap set that was negotiated at install time.

## 2. The version compatibility contract

There is no VERSION file (RFC 0006). The git tag is the std
release. Product compile is `oodac build` (LLVM IR + clang +
oodar). C99 is the oodar host runtime, not an oodac emit
backend. `emit-c` / `--backend c` residual (exit 2).

The documented contract:

  std=1.0.x         The std library release. Semver. Git tag.
  ooda=2.10.0       The minimum ooda toolchain version that works.
  ooda_max=2.99.0   The maximum ooda toolchain version that works.
  oodac=2.10.0      The minimum oodac compiler version that works.
  api_surface=8     std_mod_subdir_count() in anchor.oo is 8.

The rule is simple. std/ refuses to load if the installed ooda
toolchain is below 2.10.0, above 2.99.0, or if oodac is below
2.10.0. The api_surface check is independent of the toolchain
version: it is a property of the std release itself, and the
loader asserts it on every load.

The range [2.10.0, 2.99.0] is inclusive. 2.10.0 works. 2.99.0
works. 3.0.0 does not. If you upgrade ooda past 2.99.0, you must
also wait for the next major std release that lifts the cap. Do
not bypass this check. The cap exists to keep the public surface
stable.

## 3. What consumers should check

Before you ship a program that depends on std/, verify four things:

  1. opm show openOODA/std. The version must be 1.0.0 or a
     compatible 1.x.y patch. Different majors are not compatible.
  2. ooda --version. The version must be in [2.10.0, 2.99.0]. If
     it is not, upgrade or downgrade your toolchain to land in
     range. Pin via opm.lock and the git tag; do not invent a
     VERSION file (RFC 0006).
  3. oodac --version. Must be at least 2.10.0. Newer patch
     versions are fine.
  4. The api_surface check. Run any program that imports std/.
     If the loader reports "api_surface mismatch", your std
     install is corrupted. Reinstall with `opm install --force`.

## 4. The pin in your project

In your own opm.toml, declare the dependency explicitly:

  [dependencies]
  std = "1.0.0"

The semver range resolves to the highest 1.x.y with x=0 and y up
to the latest patch. To lock to the exact release, use:

  [dependencies]
  std = "=1.0.0"

Commit the resulting opm.lock file. The lockfile is the source of
truth for what your build actually used. Without it, two builds of
the same code can pull different std patch versions and disagree
on invariants.

